EXT. CAR PARK, SEAFRONT - FOOD TRUCK - DAY

A battered Citroën van with a hatch cut into its side, painted the colour of weak custard. "NAN'S CHIPS" is lettered above the hatch. Drizzle. Gulls. The sea has gone somewhere else for the afternoon.

Inside, PAULINE ABBOTT, 60s, wiry, hair under a net, watches the fryer as if it owes her money. Her grandchild JUNE, 17, they/them, ink on their knuckles, wipes a counter that is already clean.

                    PAULINE
You've got that look.

                    JUNE
What look?

                    PAULINE
The one your mother had before she told me about Leeds.

The hatch RATTLES. A man in a good coat, GRAHAM LOCKE, 50s, leans in, politely lost.

                    GRAHAM
Sorry. Is this Pauline's?

                    PAULINE
Depends who's asking.

                    GRAHAM
Council. Licensing. It's, well, it's about the pitch.

The fryer HISSES. Nobody speaks. This is the afternoon Pauline will remember when she is asked what went wrong.

                    JUNE
(quietly)
They've already sold it, Nan. Haven't they.

                    GRAHAM
I wouldn't say sold.

                    PAULINE
Lovely. Chips, Graham? On the house.

Her smile is perfectly fixed. She drops the basket into the oil. It SPITS.

                    GRAHAM
That's very kind, but I really shouldn't.

                    PAULINE
Oh, I insist.

                    JUNE
Nan.

                    PAULINE
It's only chips, June.

A pause. The gulls get on with it. Graham takes the paper cone and, after a moment, eats one.

                    GRAHAM
Bit good, this.

                    PAULINE
Forty years.
